WEBVTT

00:01:58.126 --> 00:02:00.040
And it's rather important

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.100
because I had done 15 years of research before it,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.528
so I had a backlog, so it was easy.

00:02:06.528 --> 00:02:08.288
It's not that I was Fidel Castro

00:02:08.288 --> 00:02:10.121
and I could talk for two hours,

00:02:10.121 --> 00:02:11.443
or Bucky Fuller.

00:02:11.443 --> 00:02:13.358
I had 15 years of stuff,

00:02:13.358 --> 00:02:15.830
and the Media Lab was about to start.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:17.630
So that was easy.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.294
But there are a couple of things

00:02:20.294 --> 00:02:21.982
about that period

00:02:21.982 --> 00:02:24.035
and about what happened that are

00:02:24.035 --> 00:02:25.890
really quite important.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.446
One is that

00:02:28.446 --> 00:02:31.342
it was a period when computers